WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
So today we are making coffee with a paper filter, a kitchen scale, and a plain kettle.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Um, begin by rinsing the filter with warm water and emptying the cup before adding any coffee.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Why do we rinse the paper filter? Rinsing removes loose paper dust and warms the cup underneath.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Our first recipe uses twenty grams of coffee. Measure the beans before grinding so each attempt starts consistently.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
The grind size changes how quickly liquid moves through the bed. A coarse grind size usually drains faster.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Keep the grind size unchanged for the first cup. We need a starting point before comparing different settings.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
You know, a steady pour helps us compare cups. Pour slowly enough that the surface stays reasonably level.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Add a little water first and wait while bubbles rise. This short resting stage is called the bloom.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Why does the coffee sometimes taste sour? We will return to that question when we discuss grinders.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
I will record the total brew time in a notebook. The number helps compare one attempt with another.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.000
And so the notebook should describe taste as well. Write whether the cup seems sharp, sweet, or dry.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
What is the best way to store beans during a humid summer? That question stays on our notebook page.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Like, the kettle does not need a fancy display. A comfortable handle matters more for this first attempt.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Temperature stability is important. It means keeping the heat similar throughout the pour, and that is all for now.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
We will use the same mug for each comparison. Different mug shapes can distract from the tasting exercise.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
You know what I mean, keep the setup simple. Change one choice at a time and write it down.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Let the drink cool slightly before tasting again. Compare the first warm sip with a later cooler sip.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
We will leave a space for notes about aroma. Simple words are enough for a useful personal record.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
We will save filters for another video. I won't go into filter materials during this short starting lesson.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Clean the kettle and scale after finishing the recipe. A tidy table makes the next practice session easier.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
For the next comparison use the same beans and dose. The notebook will then show what actually changed.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
This first cup is only a starting point for practice. Save your notes and bring them to our next lesson.
